Bluetooth: handle an unsupported Read Local Simple Pairing Options command

The MT7663S on the CloudBook Air advertises opcode 0x100c with the
factory ROM patch, then responds with Unknown HCI Command. This aborts
HCI initialization before post_init, leaving the Wi-Fi function waiting
for the Bluetooth supplier on every probe.

Treat this specific response to the optional query like an unset support
bit. Clearing the bit also keeps MGMT from reporting key information or
public key validation based on a command the controller rejected. Leave
other errors and the shared eFuse initialization ordering intact.

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
@@ -5013,11 +5013,24 @@ static int hci_read_local_codecs_sync(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 /* Read local pairing options if the HCI command is supported */
 static int hci_read_local_pairing_opts_sync(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 {
+	int err;
+
 	if (!(hdev->commands[41] & 0x08))
 		return 0;
 
-	return __hci_cmd_sync_status(hdev, HCI_OP_READ_LOCAL_PAIRING_OPTS,
-				     0, NULL, HCI_CMD_TIMEOUT);
+	err = __hci_cmd_sync_status(hdev, HCI_OP_READ_LOCAL_PAIRING_OPTS,
+				    0, NULL, HCI_CMD_TIMEOUT);
+	if (err != -EBADRQC)
+		return err;
+
+	/* Some controllers advertise this optional command but reply with
+	 * Unknown HCI Command. MGMT also consults this capability bit.
+	 */
+	hci_dev_lock(hdev);
+	hdev->commands[41] &= ~0x08;
+	hci_dev_unlock(hdev);
+	bt_dev_warn(hdev, "Read Local Simple Pairing Options advertised but unsupported");
+	return 0;
 }
 
 /* Get MWS transport configuration if the HCI command is supported */
